CKPOOL NOTIFIER SETUP
=====================

The `notifier` binary is a small, fire-and-forget utility that signals a
running ckpool instance to immediately fetch a new block template from
bitcoind and broadcast updated work to all connected miners.

It is invoked by Bitcoin Core's -blocknotify mechanism each time a new block
appears on the network, giving miners fresh work with the minimum possible
latency.  Without it, ckpool falls back to polling bitcoind at the interval
set by the `blockpoll` config key (default 100 ms).


---
HOW IT WORKS

When Bitcoin Core detects a new block it executes the command defined in
bitcoin.conf's `blocknotify` option.  The notifier binary opens the Unix
domain socket that the running ckpool stratifier process is listening on,
sends the string "update", and exits.  The stratifier responds by requesting
a new block template from bitcoind and broadcasting updated work to miners.

The socket path is constructed as:

    {socket_dir}/{name}/stratifier

where:
  socket_dir  The base socket directory.  Defaults to /tmp.
              Override with ckpool's -s flag (and match it with notifier's -s).
  name        The instance name.  Defaults to "ckpool".
              Override with ckpool's -n flag (and match it with notifier's -n).

Example default path:  /tmp/ckpool/stratifier


---
NOTIFIER OPTIONS

  -n NAME    Instance name (must match the -n flag used to start ckpool).
             Default: "ckpool" (or "ckproxy" when -p is given).
  -s DIR     Socket base directory (must match ckpool's -s flag).
             Default: /tmp
  -p         Proxy mode — sets the default name to "ckproxy" instead of "ckpool".


---
SINGLE-INSTANCE SETUP

Step 1 — Enable the notify flag in your ckpool config for the relevant btcd
entry.  This tells ckpool not to poll bitcoind for block changes:

    "btcd" : [
        {
            "url"    : "127.0.0.1:8332",
            "auth"   : "youruser",
            "pass"   : "yourpass",
            "notify" : true
        }
    ]

Step 2 — Start ckpool normally (name defaults to "ckpool"):

    src/ckpool -B -c ckpool.conf

Step 3 — Add the following to bitcoin.conf, pointing to the notifier binary:

    blocknotify=/path/to/notifier

If ckpool was started with a custom -n or -s flag, include those too:

    blocknotify=/path/to/notifier -n mypool -s /var/run/ckpool

Step 4 — Reload or restart bitcoind to pick up the blocknotify change.

After the next block is found, you should see a log line from ckpool such as:
    NOTICE: Notified stratifier of block update


---
MULTI-INSTANCE SETUP

Bitcoin Core accepts only a single blocknotify command, so when multiple
ckpool instances share the same bitcoind you need a wrapper script that calls
the notifier once per instance.

A ready-made template is provided at scripts/blocknotify-multi.sh.

Step 1 — Start each ckpool instance with a unique -n name:

    src/ckpool -B -n ckpool-lhr -c ckpool-lhr.conf
    src/ckpool -B -n ckpool-std -c ckpool-std.conf

This creates sockets at:
    /tmp/ckpool-lhr/stratifier
    /tmp/ckpool-std/stratifier

Step 2 — Copy and edit the wrapper script:

    cp scripts/blocknotify-multi.sh /usr/local/bin/blocknotify-multi.sh
    chmod +x /usr/local/bin/blocknotify-multi.sh

Edit the script and adjust the -n values to match every ckpool instance
name you are running.  Each line is independent; a failure on one does not
prevent the others from being notified.

Step 3 — Set "notify": true in each ckpool config's btcd entry (same as
the single-instance case above).

Step 4 — Point bitcoin.conf at the wrapper:

    blocknotify=/usr/local/bin/blocknotify-multi.sh

Step 5 — Reload or restart bitcoind.

If ckpool instances use a custom socket directory (-s), pass the same -s
flag to each notifier call inside the wrapper script.  Example:

    "$NOTIFIER" -n ckpool-lhr -s /var/run/ckpool || true
    "$NOTIFIER" -n ckpool-std -s /var/run/ckpool || true

The NOTIFIER environment variable in the wrapper script can be used to
override the path to the notifier binary without editing the script:

    NOTIFIER=/opt/ckpool/bin/notifier blocknotify-multi.sh


---
TROUBLESHOOTING

- "Failed to open socket"
  The socket path does not exist.  Either ckpool is not running, or the -n/-s
  flags passed to the notifier do not match those used to start ckpool.
  Check: ls /tmp/ckpool*/stratifier

- No log line after a new block
  Verify bitcoin.conf has the blocknotify line and that bitcoind was restarted
  after adding it.  Check the bitcoind debug log for blocknotify execution.

- Notifier not found
  Place the notifier binary on $PATH or use an absolute path in bitcoin.conf /
  the wrapper script.
